Routine Skin Cancer Screening (Skin Check)
General Practice Other
Comprehensive full-body skin examination, Use of dermatoscope, Identification of benign/suspicious lesions, Discussion of sun protection

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-039
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe health anxiety and panic attack following the sudden discovery of a rapidly changing, bleeding mole on the back.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient who, while showering tonight, snagged a mole on their back with a towel, causing it to bleed profusely. The patient noted the lesion was black, irregular, and had grown recently, triggering a severe panic attack and fear of immediate, advanced melanoma. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to physically manage the acute panic, achieve hemostasis, utilize a dermatoscope to differentiate a traumatized benign lesion from an obvious malignancy, and formulate an immediate surgical plan. Delaying assessment would have resulted in an unnecessary ED presentation driven by severe psychiatric distress and uncontrolled minor bleeding.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "I ripped a black mole on my back with the towel, it's bleeding everywhere, and I'm terrified it's melanoma."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents for an opportunistic, targeted skin examination driven by acute trauma to a specific lesion. The patient is currently highly anxious. An assessment of personal or family history reveals severe, blistering sunburns in adolescence but no family history of melanoma. The patient requires a systematic visual inspection of the integument, heavily focused on the bleeding lesion. The clinical presentation necessitates the use of a dermatoscope to evaluate the specific pigmented and suspicious lesion. The patient demands immediate reassurance or an immediate plan for excision.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently exhibiting acute psychomotor agitation and refusing to wear a shirt due to the bleeding back lesion.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute health anxiety; spouse is highly supportive.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 145/92 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Anxious, pacing, holding a blood-spotted towel to the right scapula.
Physical Exam: Integumentary: Examination of the right scapula reveals a 1cm, deeply pigmented, raised lesion with a partially avulsed, bleeding hyperkeratotic crust. Dermoscopy reveals classic features of a traumatized seborrheic keratosis (comedo-like openings, milia-like cysts, and a stuck-on appearance), with a complete absence of a typical pigment network, irregular dots/globules, or blue-white veil characteristic of melanoma. The encounter involves the identification of clinically benign lesions such as this seborrhoeic keratosis. Expected investigation results include an absence of atypical melanocytic proliferation on subsequent histopathology if the suspicious lesion is biopsied, which it will be for definitive reassurance.
Environmental Observations: Safe, brightly lit environment allowing for clear dermoscopic evaluation.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Routine Skin Cancer Screening (Traumatized Seborrheic Keratosis mimicking Melanoma) - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Nodular Melanoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear dermoscopic features of a benign seborrheic keratosis, despite the gross macroscopic appearance of a dark, bleeding nodule.

Pigmented Basal Cell Carcinoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of arborizing vessels or leaf-like structures on dermoscopy.

Pyogenic Granuloma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of dark pigmentation and keratin plugs, rather than a purely vascular, bright red, friable nodule.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient experienced a severe acute stress reaction after traumatizing a heavily pigmented skin lesion, catastrophizing it as a bleeding melanoma. Telephone triage correctly identified the need for immediate medical assessment to arrest the bleeding and manage the acute panic. Urgent face-to-face evaluation was mandatory to apply direct pressure/hemostatic agents to the bleeding site, and crucially, to perform an expert dermoscopic examination. By definitively identifying the lesion as a benign, "stuck-on" seborrheic keratosis, the clinician provided undeniable, objective reassurance that completely neutralized the patient's severe panic attack and prevented a highly inappropriate midnight ED presentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Applied topical aluminum chloride (hemostatic agent) and a firm pressure dressing to the right scapula, achieving complete hemostasis. Provided extensive verbal reassurance and dermoscopic education, explicitly showing the patient the benign features. Formulated a plan for an elective shave excision in-hours for cosmetic and functional reasons (preventing further snagging). Engaged in a discussion of ongoing strict sun protection measures.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maintain proactive skin health without triggering anxiety, commit to performing a structured full-body skin check in the mirror on the first day of every season (4 times a year), rather than obsessively checking randomly. Pair this check with changing the batteries in your smoke alarms. By anchoring the check to a specific, infrequent calendar event, you reduce daily obsessive anxiety while ensuring regular, systematic self-monitoring.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Sterile dressings (provided from bag).
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days to perform a formal shave excision and send the tissue for histological confirmation to provide absolute final reassurance.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Seek immediate medical attention if the lesion begins bleeding heavily through the thick dressing tonight, or if you develop rapidly spreading redness and heat around the site tomorrow.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The bleeding has completely stopped. The lesion is benign, but because it is raised and catches on your towel, the best course is to have your GP shave it off next week.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Leave the dressing completely intact tonight to ensure the clot stabilizes.
PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The mole you ripped is called a seborrheic keratosis. It is completely benign and sits on the surface of the skin like a barnacle. Because it has a lot of blood vessels, ripping the top off makes it bleed heavily, looking much scarier than it actually is.
Actionable Steps: Sleep on your stomach or side tonight to avoid pressure on the dressing. Keep the dressing completely dry. Attend your GP next week to have the rest of it cleanly removed.

